                <pb facs="0199" n="171" sameAs="frus:frus1961-63v11/pg171"/>

                <head>49. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>At 9 a.m. on October 23, 1962, Secretary of State Rusk addressed a Special
                    Meeting of the Council of the Organization of American States. After reviewing
                    the steps that had brought the situation to a head, Rusk asked the Council to
                    take all measures necessary to remove the threat to hemispheric security. For
                    text of his address, see Department of State <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Bulletin</hi>, November 12, 1962, pages 720-722. The Council continued its
                    deliberations until 5 p.m., when it passed, with one abstention, a resolution
                    along the lines called for by Rusk. For its text, see <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >American Foreign Policy, Current Documents, 1962</hi>, pages 408-410.</p>
                <p>At 4 p.m. Ambassador Stevenson delivered his opening statement to the specially
                    convened meeting of the United Nations Security Council calling the installation
                    of the missiles in Cuba a profound challenge to the peace. For text of his
                    statement and the following ones by the Cuban and Soviet representatives, see
                    U.N. doc. S/PV. 1022.</p>
            </div>
